mode: "100644"
text: "# Calendar & Cadence\n\nHow the balanced mix gets placed in time — realistically, sustainably, and in a sensible order.\n\n---\n\n## Cadence — consistency beats volume\n\nThe right cadence is the one the user can **sustain**. A consistent 3 posts/week for a year\nbeats 2 posts/day for three weeks and then silence. Start from capacity, not ambition.\n\nReasonable starting cadences (adjust to the brand and platform, and to capacity):\n\n- **Instagram / TikTok:** ~3–5/week.\n- **LinkedIn:** ~2–4/week.\n- **X / Threads:** higher volume tolerated (daily+), shorter posts.\n- **Pinterest:** steady, SEO-driven; volume helps over time.\n- **YouTube long-form:** weekly or biweekly; Shorts more often.\n\nThese are starting points, not targets to max out. Match volume to what the user can keep up.\n\n## Capacity check (do this before sizing the plan)\n\nAsk, or infer:\n\n- How many posts can they realistically create per week?\n- How much raw material exists (assets, stories, products, footage)?\n- Solo or a team? Time available?\n\nThen size the plan to that, and make up the difference with **repurposing** (one idea → several\nposts) rather than demanding more net-new ideas than they can produce. A plan the user abandons\nin week two is worse than a smaller plan they finish.\n\n## Sequencing rules\n\nOrder matters across the period, not just the count:\n\n- **Space out promotions** — never stack two asks back-to-back; cushion them with value.\n- **Vary formats day to day** — don't run three carousels in a row.\n- **Lead with strength** — open the week/month with your best hook to set the tone.\n- **Anchor recurring series** on fixed days (e.g., Tip Tuesday) so the audience learns the\n  rhythm.\n- **Cluster a campaign deliberately** when there's a launch — a few coordinated posts, still\n  buffered by value content.\n\n## Timing (advisory — not analytics-backed)\n\nSuggest posting times from general principles — post when the audience is active, respect time\nzones, weekday business hours for B2B, evenings/weekends for some consumer audiences. **State\nclearly that these are general suggestions, not the user's own best times.** WoopSocial stores\nlifetime post metrics for the covered platforms, but that is not a timing model, so the suggestion\nisn't personalized. Don't present generic timing as if it were the user's data.\n\n## The batching workflow (why this skill saves time)\n\nThe efficiency comes from doing like with like:\n\n1. **Plan the whole period** (this skill) — all slots, briefs, calendar.\n2. **Produce in batches by format** — write all captions together, script all Reels together,\n   make all graphics together. Context-switching is the tax; batching removes it.\n3. **Schedule the batch** — hand the approved set to `scheduling-and-queue` once.\n\nPlan once, make in batches, schedule once — instead of starting from zero every day.\n\n## Calendar output\n\nPresent the plan as a scannable calendar table the user can review and edit before anything is\nscheduled:\n\n| Date | Platform | Pillar | Intent | Format | Hook angle | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\nMake it editable and reviewable — the user should be able to spot and fix a wrong day, a doubled\npromo, or a format clash *before* it's committed.\n"
